# {{PROJECT_NAME}} - Specification workflow

## Sources of truth

{{SPEC_SOURCE_MAP}}

The Backlog board lives at `{{BACKLOG_DIR}}/`. Run every Backlog command from the
project root with `BACKLOG_CWD` set to that absolute root; verify
`backlog config get projectName` before mutations. Use the configured statuses,
priorities and types reported by the CLI, not assumed defaults.

Current approved behavior lives in the applicable capability baseline. An
approved active change overlays that baseline for its own scope until delivery.
A draft is a proposal. Code and tests are evidence of implementation; a conflict
with the approved contract needs a product decision or a bug fix, not silent
rewriting of either source.

## Depth and review policy

{{SPEC_POLICY}}

<!-- BEGIN: delegation -->
Use [the role contracts](agent-roles.md) under the agent guide's approved
delegation policy. Specification/design review fits before scope agreement;
delivery review fits before closure step 2 completes. These are existing review
points, not extra approval stages. The policy determines whether a separate
reviewer is invoked and whether independence is required. Record provenance and
findings against the reviewed snapshot; OpenSpec readiness never satisfies that
review requirement by itself.

<!-- END: delegation -->

| Route | Use when | Artifacts |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Direct Backlog | Work preserves a known contract, or is a discovery spike | Task according to the working agreement |
| `backlog-lean` | Behavior changes within an understood design | Proposal + delta specs |
| `backlog-design` | Architecture, trust/data boundaries, migration or significant operational choices change | Proposal + delta specs + design |

Use one short proposal and the smallest useful capability scope. Do not add an
epic, ADR or separate report just to fill a template. If design uncertainty
emerges in a lean change, keep the change ID, explicitly select `backlog-design`
in its `.openspec.yaml`, and review design before dependent implementation.

## 1. Scope the change

Read the relevant product context, baseline specs, existing tasks and actual
code. State the actor, problem, expected outcome and exclusions. For brownfield
work, distinguish observed behavior, approved baseline, intended delta and
unknowns; attach evidence to observations. Baseline only the touched capability.
For greenfield work, specify the first deliverable slice, not the whole roadmap.
Use one-level capability folders for the tested layout. On adoption of nested
capability paths, verify installed CLI archive support first; preserve the paths.

Create or resume one change, using its stable kebab-case ID:

```bash
openspec new change <change-id> --schema backlog-lean
openspec status --change <change-id> --json
openspec instructions proposal --change <change-id> --json
```

Select `backlog-design` at creation when that route applies. Check for an
existing change before creating it. The instructions command returns guidance
and a template; the agent writes the approved content.

## 2. Specify behavior, then design when needed

Write `proposal.md`: why, outcome, scope, capabilities and unresolved questions.
Then obtain and follow the artifact instructions:

```bash
openspec instructions specs --change <change-id> --json
```

- `openspec/specs/<capability>/spec.md` is the current baseline.
- `openspec/changes/<change-id>/specs/<capability>/spec.md` is the proposed delta.
- Use `### Requirement: <stable-id> <name>` and normative SHALL/MUST wording.
- Use `#### Scenario: <name>` with concrete GIVEN/WHEN/THEN observations. Each
  added or modified requirement needs at least one scenario.
- Include relevant error/denial behavior and measurable quality constraints.
  Keep capability-specific security and privacy behavior in that capability.
  Link shared rules and authoritative API/data contracts instead of copying them.
- ADDED introduces a requirement. MODIFIED replaces the **entire existing
  requirement block**, retaining its exact heading and all surviving scenarios.
  REMOVED records reason and migration; RENAMED uses OpenSpec's FROM/TO format.
- Preserve existing headings without IDs; a heading change requires an explicit
  rename. Do not put unresolved guesses into approved normative statements.

For `backlog-design`, run
`openspec instructions design --change <change-id> --json` and record the
technical approach, alternatives and relevant operational consequences in
`design.md`. Durable decisions link to the agreed ADR store. Execution steps
belong on the active Backlog task.

Run `openspec validate <change-id> --type change --strict`. Review the actual
behavior and scenarios under the policy above; CLI validation only checks
structure. Approved requirements need concrete conditions and outcomes, with no
placeholder text or blocking business decisions. Record the agreed scope in the
proposal's Agreement section.

## 3. Hand off to Backlog

After scope approval, propose focused tasks with independently testable
acceptance criteria. Reuse existing matching tasks before creating new ones.
Each task carries:

- label `change-<change-id>` and the immutable change ID in its description;
- covered requirement IDs (or exact legacy headings) and scenario names;
- repository-relative references to the existing delta files and relevant
  baseline/design files;
- an outcome, bounded scope, dependencies and evidence expected for its criteria.

Inspect the installed command help: creation uses `--labels`; editing an
existing task can use `--add-label` to preserve other labels. For example,
replacing each quoted placeholder with its approved value:

```bash
BACKLOG_CWD="$PWD" backlog task create "<title>" \
  --type "<configured-type>" --priority "<configured-priority>" \
  --status "<approved-initial-status>" --labels "change-<change-id>" \
  -d "<outcome, Change ID, requirement IDs and scenario scope>" \
  --ref "openspec/changes/<change-id>/specs/<capability>/spec.md" \
  --ac "<requirement-linked, independently observable criterion>"
```

Put each criterion in its own `--ac` argument. Record plans only after inspecting
the implementation. Create parent/dependency tasks first.
Every changed requirement/scenario must map to a task or explicit existing
verification evidence; revise missing scope before starting implementation.

Backlog owns assignment, dependencies, plans, status and evidence. There is no
OpenSpec `tasks.md`, delivery checklist or mirrored progress table. Clarifications
that change behavior must update the spec and affected task criteria together.

## 4. Implement and verify

```bash
openspec instructions apply --change <change-id> --json
BACKLOG_CWD="$PWD" backlog task list --labels change-<change-id> --json
```

Read this workflow and the linked specs before activating an eligible task.
Follow the Backlog execution/completion instructions and the agent guide. Verify
the applicable scenarios and integration boundaries; record commands, results,
test paths and relevant revision in the task's final summary. Check acceptance
criteria only when their evidence exists. An AI-generated scenario is not a test
result. Use manual evidence when appropriate and say what was actually checked.

The schemas deliberately omit `apply.tracks`. OpenSpec does not read this board.
Its `ready` state, empty task array or `0/0` progress does **not** prove delivery
or authorize closure. Read actual task details with `backlog task view <id>
--json`. If there are no linked tasks, the change is still in planning; create
approved work or keep it open. A documents-only kickoff can stop at that point.

## 5. Close the change and update the baseline

Use the following closure procedure through the project agent agreement, even
when a generic OpenSpec assistant command offers to archive automatically:

1. Enumerate all `change-<change-id>` tasks, including parents, and inspect their
   criteria and evidence. All required delivery work must have the configured
   successful completion status and passing evidence for the agreed scenarios.
   Cancelled or deferred work is not delivered; revise/split the approved delta
   first. Keep these tasks editable until reference updates are finished.
2. Check the actual diff against the approved delta/design. Reconcile any other
   active change touching the same requirement against the latest baseline.
   Validate the change with `--type change --strict` and complete the project's
   review policy. Structural validity alone is insufficient.
3. Archive once, letting the CLI merge the deltas into main specs:

   ```bash
   openspec archive <change-id> --yes
   ```

   This command does not enforce the external Backlog checks above. Do not use
   `--skip-specs` for a behavior change or `--no-validate` to bypass a conflict.
   Check the resulting files and reported archive destination, not only the exit
   code. If an earlier sync already applied the deltas, reconcile that state
   instead of applying them a second time.
4. Inspect affected main specs, preserving unrelated requirements and scenarios.
   For a new capability, replace any generated Purpose placeholder with the
   already approved purpose; do not invent behavior during this cleanup. Run
   `openspec validate <capability> --type spec --strict` for each affected spec.
5. Replace active-change references on the linked tasks with the **actual**
   archive paths through `backlog task edit --remove-ref ... --add-ref ...`, and
   add relevant canonical spec references. Preserve other task references. Fix
   relative links inside moved artifacts and inbound documentation links;
   stable change/requirement IDs remain the historical join keys. Verify links.
6. Update only cross-task product/roadmap facts and durable decisions in their
   designated owners. The archived proposal/design explain history; current
   behavior is read from the main specs.

If closure is interrupted, inspect both the active path and actual archive path,
main specs and task references before resuming. Complete missing post-archive
checks/reference updates without rerunning a successfully applied delta. Keep
an unresolved conflict visible; do not report the change closed until these
checks pass.

